## 1. The Enterprise Distribution Mental Model: Public vs. Internal Stores

In enterprise mobility, software release engineering bifurcates into two distinct delivery channels based on target audience and regulatory sensitivity:

<div class="portal-table-wrap">
  <table class="portal-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Release Vector</th>
        <th>Internal Enterprise App Store (Private MDM)</th>
        <th>Public App Stores (Apple App Store &amp; Google Play)</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Target Audience</strong></td>
        <td>Enterprise employees, field operators, contractors, and internal business stakeholders.</td>
        <td>Public consumers, commercial partners, farmers, dealers, and external clients.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Access Control</strong></td>
        <td>Restricted by corporate identity (Okta / Entra ID). Business applications require entitlement approval via <code>access.enterprise.internal</code>.</td>
        <td>Publicly discoverable worldwide or geo-gated by country on Google Play and Apple App Store.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Review &amp; Approval</strong></td>
        <td>Internal Technology Governance &amp; Regulatory Compliance (TGRC) security sign-off. Zero external vendor review.</td>
        <td>Apple App Review Board (1–2 weeks turnaround) and Google Play Review team (~1 week turnaround).</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Signing Certificates</strong></td>
        <td>iOS Enterprise Developer Certificate (In-House Distribution) and unified enterprise Android Release Keystore.</td>
        <td>Apple Developer Program Distribution Certificate and Google Play App Signing (Play-managed root keys).</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Deployment Velocity</strong></td>
        <td>Instant publishing upon pipeline completion; updates propagate immediately to user devices.</td>
        <td>Subject to app store ingestion pipelines, review queues, and staged phased rollout windows.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Device Management</strong></td>
        <td>Full Mobile Device Management (MDM / MAM) integration supporting remote data wipe on lost/stolen hardware.</td>
        <td>Standard consumer OS sandboxing; zero enterprise remote wipe capability outside application-level logic.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

