## 3. iOS Enterprise Certificate Trust Configuration

On iOS devices, enterprise applications signed with an in-house **Apple Developer Enterprise Program Certificate** are not trusted by default by the operating system until the employee manually authorizes the root organization certificate:

<div class="portal-alert-box warning">
  <div class="portal-alert-title">MANDATORY IOS FIRST-LAUNCH TRUST CONFIGURATION</div>
  <p>
    If an employee opens an enterprise app before authorizing the certificate, iOS displays: <strong>"Untrusted Enterprise Developer: Your device management profile has not been trusted on this iPhone."</strong> Users must execute the following one-time approval runbook:
  </p>
</div>

### iOS 16, 17, and 18 Certificate Trust Runbook:

1. Open the device **Settings** app.
2. Navigate to **General &rarr; VPN &amp; Device Management** (on older iOS versions: *General &rarr; Device Management* or *General &rarr; Profiles &amp; Device Management*).
3. Under the **Enterprise App** section, tap the enterprise organization name: `Enterprise Global Services Inc.`.
4. Tap **Trust "Enterprise Global Services Inc."**.
5. When the confirmation prompt appears (*"Trusting will allow any app from this enterprise developer to be used on your iPhone and may allow access to your data"*), tap **Trust**.
6. Return to the home screen; the Enterprise App Store and all hosted enterprise applications will launch immediately.

