## 1. Application Signing Architecture: Android & iOS

To safeguard binary integrity and prevent unauthorized code injection, all mobile builds must be cryptographically signed using enterprise-governed digital certificates before deployment:

<div class="portal-table-wrap">
  <table class="portal-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Platform</th>
        <th>Certificate Type</th>
        <th>Target Distribution Pathway</th>
        <th>Security Governance Standard</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Android</strong></td>
        <td>Unified Enterprise Release Keystore (<code>.jks</code>)</td>
        <td>Enterprise App Store (QA/UAT/PROD) <strong>and</strong> Public Google Play Store.</td>
        <td>The mobile engineering team maintains <strong>one unified enterprise certificate</strong> utilized to sign both internal enterprise and public customer-facing applications.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>iOS (Internal)</strong></td>
        <td>Apple Developer Enterprise Program Certificate (In-House Distribution)</td>
        <td>Enterprise App Store (QA, UAT, and PROD).</td>
        <td>Signed using enterprise distribution licenses for direct employee deployment without Apple review. Requires manual device certificate trust in iOS Settings.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>iOS (Public)</strong></td>
        <td>Apple Developer Program Distribution Certificate</td>
        <td>Public Apple App Store and TestFlight.</td>
        <td>Signed using the public commercial developer account. Submissions are subject to mandatory Apple App Review guidelines and review boards.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

### Application ID Naming Convention:
All enterprise mobile applications across both Android and iOS must adhere to the standardized reverse-domain naming convention:
```text
com.enterprise.<appname>
```
*Examples:*
* `com.enterprise.graindesk`
* `com.enterprise.driverconnect`
* `com.enterprise.plantops`

